• It is hard to define – what one person sees as a harmless joke or compliment, another experiences as offensive; it depends on the victim''s perception (e.g. artwork or jokes that make some employees uncomfortable).
• Power differences – it often involves a supervisor and a subordinate (quid pro quo), so victims fear retaliation, losing their job or promotion and often don''t report it.
• Hostile work environment – it is not only about direct demands; offensive comments, pictures or behavior by coworkers can also create a hostile environment.
• Legal and financial risk – organizations can be held liable, especially if they knew about it and did nothing; settlements can be enormous, plus damage to reputation and morale.
• It harms people and performance – lower productivity, absenteeism, turnover.

Managers must therefore educate employees, have a clear policy and complaint procedure, investigate promptly, protect the accused''s right to appeal (to a higher manager not involved in the case) and act to stop it.'),
('c8-o2', 8, 2, 'In a short essay, discuss how workforce diversity is directly affected by recruitment, selection, orientation, and training.', 'Workforce diversity – the ways people differ and are similar (age, gender, race, ethnicity, religion, disability, etc.) – is directly shaped by the HRM process:

• Recruitment – to increase diversity, managers must widen their recruiting net beyond referrals from current employees (which tend to reproduce the existing workforce), e.g. advertise in ethnic newspapers, target diverse universities and organizations, use diverse online channels.
• Selection – the selection process must be fair and non-discriminatory: use valid, job-related criteria (job analysis, specifications), structured interviews and diverse interview panels; follow equal employment opportunity laws and affirmative action where required, so qualified diverse candidates are actually hired.
• Orientation – helps diverse new hires adapt and feel included: introduce the culture, mentors, employee networks; signals that diversity is valued, which reduces early turnover.
• Training – diversity awareness and skills training help all employees understand and work effectively with people who are different, reduce bias and prevent harassment and discrimination.

Together these steps determine who joins the organization, who stays and how well a diverse workforce works together.')
